\begin{align*}
AB^2 &= AE^2 + BE^2\\
\end{align*}

**Given that $BE = 9$ units:**

\begin{align*}
AB^2 &= AE^2 + 9^2\\
AB^2 &= AE^2 + 81
\end{align*}

**Since $AB$ is the hypotenuse, the squares on it are the sum of the squares on $AE$ and $BE$. Thus, the area of $ABCD$ and $AEFG$ are:**

\begin{align*}
\text{Area(ABCD)} &= AB^2\\
\text{Area(AEFG)} &= AE^2 + 81
\end{align*}

**But we know that the area is the square of the side length, so:**

\begin{align*}
\text{Area(ABCD)} &= AB^2\\
\text{Area(AEFG)} &= AB^2 - 81
\end{align*}

**The sum of the areas is then:**

\begin{align*}
\text{Area(ABCD)} - \text{Area(AEFG)} &= 81\\
\end{align*}

**However, this does not make sense since we're looking for the sum of the areas, not the difference. The correct approach is to consider that the sum of the areas of $ABCD$ and $AEFG$ equals $AB^2 + (AE^2 + 81)$, which is just the square of $AB$ plus the square of $BE$:**

\begin{align*}
\text{Area(ABCD)} + \text{Area(AEFG)} &= AB^2 + (AE^2 + 81)\\
&= (AB^2 + AE^2) + 81\\
&= 81 + 81\\
&= 162
\end{align*}

**Thus, the sum of the areas of the two squares $ABCD$ and $AEFG$ is $162$ square units.**

